<%#
App-level override of the react_on_rails_pro engine's rsc_payload template
(issue #245 — read-your-writes spike).

Plain Rails view-path precedence makes this file shadow the gem's
app/views/react_on_rails_pro/rsc_payload.text.erb (the engine has no
isolate_namespace and no view-path manipulation). The stock template calls
rsc_payload_react_component with NO async-props block, so an async-props page
refetched through RSCRoute.refetch() renders without getReactOnRailsAsyncProp
in its props and every async section throws
"TypeError: getReactOnRailsAsyncProp is not a function"
(evidence: tmp/spike-245-evidence/config1-stock, docs/rsc-read-your-writes.md).

This override staffs that door: async-props pages dispatch by component name
to the same shared emitter the initial-render view uses, so both doors stream
identical props. Everything else keeps the stock plain-props path.

Gotchas:
 * Must stay `.text.erb` — RSCPayloadRenderer renders with formats: [:text]
   and raises a bespoke MissingTemplate error for `.html.erb` overrides.
 * Every tag below uses trailing-newline trims (and this comment must never
   contain a literal ERB close sequence): the response is an NDJSON stream
   and stray template whitespace would corrupt the length-prefixed chunks.
 * @rsc_payload_component_props comes from the request query string
   (?props=...), i.e. FROM THE BROWSER. This override therefore reads ONLY the
   product id out of it, 404s on an unknown/missing id before any chunk is
   emitted, and rebuilds the initial props server-side from the found record
   (ProductRscProps.initial_props) instead of echoing the browser's copy. A
   real app must still authorize the request — the 17.1 gem adds
   rsc_payload_authorizer for exactly this seam.
-%>
<% if @rsc_payload_component_name == "ProductPageRSC" -%>
<%
  # @payload_product was validated and loaded by RscPayloadController's
  # before_action, which 404s BEFORE the stream starts — no re-parse or
  # re-query of the untrusted props here. The raise is defense in depth for a
  # mis-wired route only (it would surface as ActionView::Template::Error
  # mid-stream, not a 404).
  if @payload_product.nil?
    raise ActiveRecord::RecordNotFound,
          "ProductPageRSC payload requires RscPayloadController to load @payload_product"
  end
-%>
<%= rsc_payload_react_component_with_async_props(
      @rsc_payload_component_name,
      props: ProductRscProps.initial_props(@payload_product)
    ) do |emit|
      ProductRscProps.emit_all(@payload_product, emit)
    end -%>
<% elsif @rsc_payload_component_name == "ProductPageRSCForms" -%>
<%
  # Issue #244: form-library comparison page. Same trust posture and emitter as
  # the ProductPageRSC branch — same product data, same async props.
  if @payload_product.nil?
    raise ActiveRecord::RecordNotFound,
          "ProductPageRSCForms payload requires RscPayloadController to load @payload_product"
  end
-%>
<%= rsc_payload_react_component_with_async_props(
      @rsc_payload_component_name,
      props: ProductRscProps.initial_props(@payload_product)
    ) do |emit|
      ProductRscProps.emit_all(@payload_product, emit)
    end -%>
<% elsif @rsc_payload_component_name == "ProductReviewsSectionRSC" -%>
<%
  # C5 (issue #245): section-scoped refetch door. Same trust posture as the
  # ProductPageRSC branch — the controller's before_action validated the
  # browser props and loaded @payload_product before any chunk streamed; the
  # narrow emitter re-streams ONLY review_stats + reviews.
  if @payload_product.nil?
    raise ActiveRecord::RecordNotFound,
          "ProductReviewsSectionRSC payload requires RscPayloadController to load @payload_product"
  end
-%>
<%= rsc_payload_react_component_with_async_props(
      @rsc_payload_component_name,
      props: ProductRscProps.section_initial_props(@payload_product)
    ) do |emit|
      ProductRscProps.emit_reviews_section(@payload_product, emit)
    end -%>
<% else -%>
<%= rsc_payload_react_component(@rsc_payload_component_name, props: @rsc_payload_component_props) -%>
<% end -%>
